UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 16, Lesson 1: What makes a good friend', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A good friend is kind, honest, reliable and respectful. Good friends listen, share, include others, say sorry and help each other. Friendships can change, and people can have many types of friends. Respectful friends accept ''no'' and do not pressure, threaten or leave people out. Anyone who makes you feel unsafe is not acting like a good friend, and you can tell a trusted adult.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA friend says, ''If you don''t give me your snack, I won''t be your friend.'' Is this respectful? Use two friendship qualities in your answer.\n\nFEEDBACK\nNot respectful: it pressures and threatens. A respectful friend accepts a ''no'', which shows respect and kindness. You can say ''No thanks'' and tell a trusted adult if it keeps happening.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking friends must always agree and do everything together. Friends can disagree, have different interests and still be kind and respectful.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWrite a description of a made-up good friend with five qualities and a real-life style example for each. Add two signs that a friendship is not respectful and name one trusted adult you could tell.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"A good friend is kind, honest, reliable and respectful. Good friends listen, share, include others, say sorry and help each other. Friendships can change, and people can have many types of friends. Respectful friends accept ''no'' and do not pressure, threaten or leave people out. Anyone who makes you feel unsafe is not acting like a good friend, and you can tell a trusted adult.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Learn and discuss","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-RRS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Write a description of a made-up good friend with five qualities and a real-life style example for each. Add two signs that a friendship is not respectful and name one trusted adult you could tell. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Not respectful: it pressures and threatens. A respectful friend accepts a ''no'', which shows respect and kindness. You can say ''No thanks'' and tell a trusted adult if it keeps happening. Reasoning: I used a good friend by attending to kindness rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking friends must always agree and do everything together. Friends can disagree, have different interests and still be kind and respectful. Correction: Not respectful: it pressures and threatens. A respectful friend accepts a ''no'', which shows respect and kindness. You can say ''No thanks'' and tell a trusted… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a good friend.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking friends must always agree and do everything together. Friends can disagree, have different interests and still be kind and respectful. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a good friend on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a good friend, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Not respectful: it pressures and threatens. A respectful friend accepts a ''no'', which shows respect and kindness. You can say ''No thanks'' and tell a trusted… This matches the lesson skill for a good friend.","options":["Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Not respectful: it pressures and threatens. A respectful friend accepts a ''no'', which shows respect and kindness. You can say ''No thanks'' and tell a trusted…","Thinking friends must always agree and do everything together. Friends can disagree, have different interests and still be kind and respectful."],"question":"A friend says, ''If you don''t give me your snack, I won''t be your friend.'' Is this respectful? Use two friendship qualities in your answer. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Thinking friends must always agree and do everything together. Friends can disagree, have different interests and still be kind and respectful. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["A good friend is kind, honest, reliable and respectful. Good friends listen, share, include others, say sorry and help each other. Friendships can change, and…","Not respectful: it pressures and threatens. A respectful friend accepts a ''no'', which shows respect and kindness. You can say ''No thanks'' and tell a trusted…","Careful use of kindness with clear evidence.","Thinking friends must always agree and do everything together. Friends can disagree, have different interests and still be kind and respectful."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising a good friend?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named kindness, not by copying the model.","options":["Apply the idea with new evidence and name the kindness used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter."],"question":"A learner is practising a good friend. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Not respectful: it pressures and threatens. A respectful friend accepts a ''no'', which shows respect and kindness. You can say ''No thanks'' and tell a trusted… uses safe, healthy, respectful choices appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Thinking friends must always agree and do everything together. Friends can disagree, have different interests and still be kind and respectful.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Not respectful: it pressures and threatens. A respectful friend accepts a ''no'', which shows respect and kindness. You can say ''No thanks'' and tell a trusted…"],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about a good friend?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Thinking friends must always agree and do everything together. Friends can disagree, have different interests and still be kind and respectful.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about a good friend.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about kindness?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Example: Ari lists friend qualities with real examples. Kind: shared his pencil. Honest: said my drawing needed a title but liked the…","Thinking friends must always agree and do everything together. Friends can disagree, have different interests and still be kind and respectful."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — A good friend is kind, honest, reliable and respectful. Good friends listen, share, include others, say… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Write a description of a made-up good friend with five qualities and a real-life style example for each. Add two signs that a friendship is not…","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Thinking friends must always agree and do everything together. Friends can disagree, have different interests and still be kind and respectful."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of a good friend?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a good friend on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Not respectful: it pressures and threatens. A respectful friend accepts a ''no'', which shows respect and kindness. You can say ''No thanks'' and tell a trusted adult if it keeps happening. Reasoning: I used a good friend by attending to kindness rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with a good friend, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking friends must always agree and do everything together. Friends can disagree, have different interests and still be kind and respectful. Correction: Not respectful: it pressures and threatens. A respectful friend accepts a ''no'', which shows respect and kindness. You can say ''No thanks'' and tell a trusted… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a good friend.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w16-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a good friend.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Health, Wellbeing and Relationships","success_criteria":["I can use a good friend with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify kindness and explain why it matters.","I can practise honesty independently on a new prompt.","I can notice respect needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A good friend is kind, honest, reliable and respectful. Good friends listen, share, include others, say sorry and help each other. Friendships can change, and people can have many types of friends. Respectful friends accept ''no'' and do not pressure, threaten or leave people…","options":["A good friend is kind, honest, reliable and respectful. Good friends listen, share, include others, say sorry and help each other. Friendships can change, and people can have many types of friends. Respectful friends accept ''no'' and do not pressure, threaten or leave people out. Anyone who makes you feel unsafe is not acting like a good friend, and you can tell a trusted adult.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a good friend?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Ari lists friend qualities with real examples. Kind: shared his pencil. Honest: said my drawing needed a title but liked the colours. Reliable: turned up to help at recess. Respectful: accepted ''no'' when I wanted to play alone. Each quality has an example.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA friend says, ''If you don''t give me your snack, I won''t be your friend.'' Is this respectful? Use two friendship qualities in your answer.\n\nFEEDBACK\nNot respectful: it pressures and threatens. A respectful friend accepts a ''no'', which shows respect and kindness. You can say ''No thanks'' and tell a trusted adult if it keeps happening.","explain":"A good friend is kind, honest, reliable and respectful. Good friends listen, share, include others, say sorry and help each other. Friendships can change, and people can have many types of friends. Respectful friends accept ''no'' and do not pressure, threaten or leave people out. Anyone who makes you feel unsafe is not acting like a good friend, and you can tell a trusted adult.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking friends must always agree and do everything together. Friends can disagree, have different interests and still be kind and respectful. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Good friend qualities.","caption":"Name an example.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Qualities</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">kind, honest, reliable</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Respect</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">accepts no</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Ari lists friend qualities with real examples. Kind: shared his pencil. Honest: said my drawing needed a title  Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Ari lists friend qualities with real examples. Kind: shared his pencil. Honest: said my drawing needed a title  Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a good friend?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Ari lists friend qualities with real examples. Kind: shared his pencil. Honest: said my drawing needed a title but liked the colours. Reliable: turned up to help at recess. Respectful: accepted ''no'' when I wanted to play alone. Each quality has an example. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"Pressure and respect.","caption":"Tell an adult if unsure.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Pressure</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">give me your snack or else</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Respect</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">a no is accepted</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a good friend?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking friends must always agree and do everything together. Friends can disagree, have different interests and still be kind and respectful. Here is a more helpful example: Example: Ari lists friend qualities with real examples. Kind: shared his pencil. Honest: said my drawing needed a title but liked the colours. Reliable: turned up to help at recess. Respectful: accepted ''no'' when I wanted to play alone. Each quality has an example.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nNot respectful: it pressures and threatens. A respectful friend accepts a ''no'', which shows respect and kindness. You can say ''No thanks'' and tell a trusted adult if it keeps happening.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Thinking friends must always agree and do everything together. Friends can disagree, have different interests and still be kind and respectful. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["A guess with no reason.","Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a good friend?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA good friend is kind, honest, reliable and respectful. Good friends listen, share, include others, say sorry and help each other. Friendships can change, and people can have many types of friends. Respectful friends accept ''no'' and do not pressure, threaten or leave people out. Anyone who makes you feel unsafe is not acting like a good friend, and you can tell a trusted adult. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 16, Lesson 1: What makes a good friend","unit_number":4,"unit_title":"Friendships and respect","week":16,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: Ari lists friend qualities with real examples. Kind: shared his pencil. Honest: said my drawing needed a title but liked the colours. Reliable: turned up to help at recess. Respectful: accepted ''no'' when I wanted to play alone. Each quality has an example.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Good friend qualities.","caption":"Name an example.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Qualities</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">kind, honest, reliable</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Respect</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">accepts no</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Pressure and respect.","caption":"Tell an adult if unsure.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"